@page "/docs/registry/search"
<PageTitle>Registry search - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Search</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">opt-in for servers</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">Every search works against every registry. A static registry hands over its catalog and the CLI filters it locally, while a registry with a server behind it can answer the search itself, and the CLI detects that and uses its results. Neither side needs to be told which kind it is talking to.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Searching</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/SearchCommands" />
        <p><code>-q</code> matches name, title and description, with alternatives separated by commas. <code>-t</code> keeps only the listed item types, with or without their <code>registry:</code> prefix. <code>--category</code> keeps items carrying any of the listed category tags. <code>-l</code> and <code>--offset</code> page the result. Under <code>--json</code> the output carries a <code>pagination</code> object, so scripts read totals instead of counting.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">The protocol</h2>
        <p>The search is sent as query parameters on the catalog request, so it uses the same URL and the same credentials, with nothing new to route:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/SearchRequest" />
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>Parameter</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Meaning</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>q</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The query. How it matches (substring, fuzzy, an index) is the server's choice, and the CLI does not second-guess it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>type</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Item types to keep, comma-separated wire names (<code>registry:ui,registry:theme</code>).</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>category</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Category tags to keep, comma-separated. An item matching any of them stays.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>limit</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Page size.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>offset</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>Matched items to skip.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>All parameters are optional, and a request without <code>q</code> or <code>type</code> is the plain catalog. The response is an ordinary <code>index.json</code> plus <code>pagination</code>: <code>total</code>, <code>offset</code>, <code>limit</code>, <code>hasMore</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">How the CLI decides</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b><code>pagination</code> present:</b> the registry filtered. Its items are shown as they are, because filtering again locally could drop matches a smarter server made, and its totals drive the "more" line.</li>
            <li><b><code>pagination</code> absent:</b> a static registry returned everything, and the CLI filters and pages what came back. This is every static host today, unchanged.</li>
            <li><b>Several registries at once:</b> each is asked with the query and type, and the page is applied locally over the merged list, because no single registry can page a merged list it cannot see.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Serving it</h2>
        <p>Implement server-side search only when the catalog is big enough to be worth it. A static registry already returns correct results, it just sends more data. A dynamic registry reads the parameters, filters however it likes, honors <code>limit</code> and <code>offset</code>, and writes the <code>pagination</code> object. That object is the whole handshake, with no version negotiation and no capability endpoint.</p>
        <p>Keep an empty request cheap, since <CmdChip Command="blaizio search" /> sends one when run with no flags and the interactive component picker reads it too. Authenticated registries scope results by the caller's credential exactly as they do for items (see <a href="docs/registry/authentication">Authentication</a>).</p>
    </section>
</div>
